+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. A value is either a whole
+number of seconds, like `120`, or one or more integer amounts each followed by a unit: `d` for days, `h` for hours,
+`m` for minutes and `s` for seconds. Each unit may appear at most once, in that order, without spaces. Units are
+case-insensitive, so `2d`, `1h30m`, `45S` and `1d2h3m4s` are all valid. Negative and decimal values are rejected.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting for {timeout}")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+    println()
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
+```{versionadded} 8.6.0
+```
+
